    /// <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qpointer.html">C++ documentation</a>:<div style='border: 1px solid #5CFF95; background: #D6FFE4; padding: 16px;'><p>A guarded pointer, QPointer&lt;T&gt;, behaves like a normal C++ pointer <code translate="no">T *</code>, except that it is automatically cleared when the referenced object is destroyed (unlike normal C++ pointers, which become "dangling pointers" in such cases). <code translate="no">T</code> must be a subclass of <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qobject.html" translate="no">QObject</a>.</p>
    /// <p>Guarded pointers are useful whenever you need to store a pointer to a <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qobject.html" translate="no">QObject</a> that is owned by someone else, and therefore might be destroyed while you still hold a reference to it. You can safely test the pointer for validity.</p>
