    /// <p>For creating guarded pointers, you can construct or assign to them from a T* or from another guarded pointer of the same type. You can compare them with each other using operator==() and operator!=(), or test for <code translate="no">nullptr</code> with <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qpointer.html#isNull" translate="no">isNull</a>(). You can dereference them using either the <code translate="no">*x</code> or the <code translate="no">x-&gt;member</code> notation.</p>
    /// <p>A guarded pointer will automatically cast to a <code translate="no">T</code> *, so you can freely mix guarded and unguarded pointers. This means that if you have a QPointer&lt;<a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qwidget.html" translate="no">QWidget</a>&gt;, you can pass it to a function that requires a <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qwidget.html" translate="no">QWidget</a> *. For this reason, it is of little value to declare functions to take a QPointer as a parameter; just use normal pointers. Use a QPointer when you are storing a pointer over time.</p>
